'''''Cistus ocymoides''''' ([[synonym (taxonomy)|syn.]] ''Halimium ocymoides''), the '''basil-leaved rock rose''', is a species of [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Cistaceae]], [[native plant|native]] to Portugal and Spain in the [[Iberian Peninsula]], and northern Morocco in Northwest Africa.<ref>{{cite web |title=Halimium ocymoides |url=http://www.floraiberica.es/floraiberica/texto/pdfs/03_066_02_Halimium.pdf |publisher=[[Flora Iberica]] |access-date=1 March 2022}}</ref> It is an erect [[evergreen]] shrub growing to {{convert|60|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall by {{convert|100|cm|ft|0|abbr=on}} wide, with woolly grey-green leaves and bright yellow flowers in spring. The flowers may have a dark brown blotch at the base of each petal.<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=1405332964|pages=1136}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=''Halimium ocymoides''|url=http://www.cistuspage.org.uk/Halimium%20ocymoides.htm|work=The Cistus & Halimium website|access-date=14 July 2013|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131006091950/http://www.cistuspage.org.uk/Halimium%20ocymoides.htm|archive-date=6 October 2013|df=}}</ref>

In cultivation this plant requires a sandy soil and full sun.<ref>{{cite web|title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Halimium ocymoides''|url=http://apps.rhs.org.uk/plantselector/plant?plantid=4485|accessdate=14 July 2013}}</ref>

